Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon and  thanks for coming out to metro air  support today we're glad to have all of  you  we're going to go over three basic  topics today we're going to talk to  what we respond to out at air support  search and rescue  we're going to go over some 2020  statistics and then we're going to  talk about our top five to stay alive  search and rescue campaign  that we've been really hard on the last  two years we're committed to it it's on  our banner  above our door that all the pilots and  search and rescue officers  go in and out of every day and those are  just some  tips that will help you be more  successful in the  in the wild when you go recreate so  the first thing we do the most primary  thing we do is patrol support  so basically our helicopters this md530f  is used just like a  patrol car but it's in the sky flying  around the valley at 500 feet  a lot of times that's the helicopter you  hear it looks like it's flying low it's  always flying between four and six  hundred feet  we do search and rescue missions  obviously we do the  the rescues that are beyond the  capabilities of other agencies  so fire department responds to a lot of  them out at red rock  blm will respond national park service  respond  for the ones that we can allow them to  do  they're usually less than one mile that  the fire department or blm is going  and we're doing the more complex rescues  that require helicopter back in the  canyons  we respond to mass casualty and critical  incidents they  may require a tactical team be deployed  crime scene documentation we do that  with our cis  that has minimized a little bit since  we've implemented uh the drone  program out of our technical analytical  section they've taken over some of the  more  static things we do we still handle all  of the pursuits  and the critical dynamics stuff and the  drone  basically is used for crime scene  documentation right now  mountaintop radio repeater site  maintenance  a lot of people don't know and a lot of  other agencies don't know  we are the helicopters that maintain  those repeater sites and keep our radios  functioning  we take gas water and maintenance  personnel up to those mountain top  repeater sites  the next thing we'll talk about is some  of our 2020 statistics  so last year we flew 4316  incident-free flight hours with 84  percent  or 3 597 hours being in direct  patrol support responding to police  calls  crimes happening in the valley or  proactive  enforcement we did 110  search and rescue responses we recovered  eight dead bodies  that can be in the lake we do all of the  dive operations  for the national park service they will  locate a body  with their side scan sonar boats  and we are the ones that do the dive our  public safety divers  from las vegas metro do those body  recoveries  we do them on land as well in complex  areas if people fall off a cliff and die  and they're not found for some time  we also do those body recoveries with  the helicopters  we had 236 tactical medical responses  for our swat team  and other valley-wide swat teams where  our tactical  medics respond as the medical team  to handle any injuries or wounds that  come up during those swat call outs  our patrol helicopter crews made 2498  suspect observation and crime  observations  contributing to 994 arrests  via the helicopter  um last year in 2020 we averaged 22  calls per day in the helicopter  so it's quite a bit of work we're doing  up there  now on to our top five to stay alive  life-saving tips  for in the wild the most important thing  seems pretty simple and that is  simply telling somebody where you intend  to go hike or climb  and we recommend not going alone take  somebody with you  some of the rescues we have people are  out there alone they didn't tell anybody  where they go  somebody finds a car and we have to do a  massive  search in addition to the rescue we  later have to perform  checking the weather forecast is really  important for the area you intend  to hike or climb if you know it's going  to be windy  raining snowing you need to take that  into consideration for how you prepare  and what your skill level is  the third thing is to make sure to take  food water and a first aid kit  you know sometimes just a small backpack  with food a couple bottles of water some  granola bars  and a real brief tape first aid kit  that will suffice it will get you  through the night  take the appropriate gear in minimal  overnight provisions  always plan when you go out hiking that  something bad may happen  and you may have to spend the night  because you might not meet our criteria  for responding on a night helicopter  rescue  and the last thing is is to have a light  source  a charged phone and to consider a  personal locator beacon  if you're doing very remote or very  complex hikes or climbs  where the area is not well known to  a lot of people might be known to my  team but it might not be known to the  average person  so nobody else will be able to come get  you  personal locator beacons can be bought  at like bass pro shops or something like  that  uh basically any electronics store will  have them it's a good tool to have if  you do really extreme stuff  so with that that's our our basic  presentation  does anybody have any questions  can you guys go out to rescues when  people are you know hiking camping all  these things what are some of the more  common  things that lead to the rescues and how  can people try to prevent  we basically go off of three things  right people are either lost  stuck or hurt so they get lost we go on  a search for them we try to get them  back on trail  sometimes we have to recover them in the  helicopter sometimes we can send a  ground team on one of the atvs  and we can get them back without having  to deploy a helicopter  stuck is what we refer to technically as  being ledged out you climb up somewhere  which it's most often more often than  not it's easier to go up than it is to  come down so people will climb up  a rock structure and won't be able to  get down so they'll be ledged out  they will call us 9-1-1 and say i'm up  here but i can't get down  we evaluate those rescues on how  the where the area they're stuck in how  dangerous that is to them if they're on  a two foot ledge  whether it's day or night we're probably  going to come get you right away  if you're on a 10 foot you know base and  it's real nice and flat and you can  put your tent up that you took your  overnight provisions and you can wait  till the morning  we will defer that since you're not  injured we will defer that to a daytime  helicopter rescue so it's more safe for  our pilots  and then injuries you know we get ankle  injuries we get people fall  their cleats come out of the mountain  and they fall 40 feet and they  crack their skull so we run the whole  gamut from  fatality to no injury someone just being  stuck  ledged out and then all of the ankles  arms  broken legs we do it all  the time  it's actually not it's down right now  probably due to covid  uh the parks were closed for a while  like about eight months  the parks were closed completely so  people weren't allowed to go out there  legally some people still went out there  but  we've not seen a large increase  now we're actually seeing a big  reduction from last year so far we're  only at 27  so far this year but we have implemented  like i said in the last two years we've  implemented a lot of proactive sar  where those cards i have being handed  out to you when we're done  we hand those out we go to the parking  lots we have two search and rescue  officers right now out hiking  in red rock calico basin and they're  handing out those cards to people  to make sure everyone knows the basic  things that can help you  be successful when you go out to have a  good time in the park  anything else what other things do you  use to prepare for this season do you  increase the amount of fuel you have you  increase the number of bandages do  well you have to remember we're  responsible for rescue so we're always  ready  all of my men assigned in the search and  rescue section are in extremely  top level fitness top level because  like i said when i was speaking earlier  we have to do the rescues  that nobody else can do because we're  the only  helicopter rescue company in town nobody  else has a rescue helicopter  the medevac helicopters are merely for  transport  from the scene to the hospital they're  not to go into the wild the canyons  back in and they don't even have a hoist  on them like you see this hoist is  on the h-145 helicopter the baskets  there  they don't have that so we're we're  always ready  24 7 365 we're ready  and when no one else can get you las  vegas metro police has to get you that's  our job we're required the sheriff  is responsible for that and thankfully  sheriff lombardo knows the importance of  the unit  and he was a prior section commander he  used to be in my position  and he really understands the importance  of this unit  compare this summer to two summers ago  what has coca changed about your  responsibilities  just protocol in the helicopter we do  have dispatch ask the questions if you  call  we will ask you if you have the symptoms  we do mask our victims up  when our rescue team is down on the  ground packaging them  we do require them to mask up just minor  protocol stuff  with that  no we haven't seen that that was uh her  question we have not seen an  increase in rescues this year we're  right on about schedule and this is  typically  when we're coming into our busy season  spring and summer so that's why we're  here today to give those  those tips out so hopefully we can get  fewer rescues  because our rescue team we're just  response based right we're not out  there's  a little bit we could do to prevent some  of them by educating the public  but there's not a lot we can do to  prevent you from calling if you're out  on the rock  and you get lost stuck or hurt or you're  worried or you're scared  it's getting dark on you you don't have  a light when you call 9-1-1  we assess that me personally i assess  that and i decide how we're going to  respond  when we're going to respond and who's  going to be in the helicopter when we do  at any respond they call 9-1-1 there's  not a specific number for you guys  there's not we we want them to go  through 9-1-1 because obviously you know  metro  is the hub for all 9-1-1 our dispatchers  know our protocols so they can screen  some of those calls and we might be able  to send fire department first  fire department gets out there if they  can handle it it's less than a mile  it's not in any technical terrain and  they don't need us  if blm and fire department can handle it  we're okay  with that when it gets beyond their  capabilities that's when we get the call  and then our dispatchers will notify us  all right everybody

